# 4.1 - Rally-Ho!
I might come across as a broken record, but with clients being able to act acordingly to their level in our hands, the realy tricky part comes now, as there's no more delaying the Rallying stuff. It might be PTSD talking (writing), but this almost broke my spirit. Conceptually, things are more or less easily feasable, and that acquired initial step of thinking about what Rallying means and needs comes and goes fast. Then, adjusting the increasingly more complex state machine... Well, that's the work, there are the hours, my tears are all collected down the neverending pitfalls that live in that darkness. I write this from the other side, with an already finished AI client that, while having ample room for improvement and refinement, works perfectly fine to the best of my knowledge. I write this, too, after dozens of tweaks and probes, after reading an instream of thousands of log lines, after finally achieving cooperative, team-focused, game-winning clients. All this to say that my record may be broken, but that I'm extremely sure that this can be considered the core challenge of this development process. So let's tread with extreme caution so as to not get ourselves lost in our own sauce.

With our focus in the conceptual level, let's jot down what a Rally means and needs in the context of *Zappy*. First, a situation description. Say a client at level 4 has found and picked up the necessary resources for an incantation, which according to the rules are 1 linemate, 1 deraumere, 2 sibur, 1 phiras. Our little dude has been roaming the game arena, keeping itself fed, looking for stones, growing its pockets, and has realized that there is no more need for stone collecting and the time for incantation has arrived. Now, as per the same rules, the 4->5 ritual needs 4 level 4 players to concurr in the same tile where the specified resources are placed, so our little dude needs tools to make that happen. If he's the first one to reach this situation, he'll want to tell other level 4 peers that he is in fact reached that point, and those peers would need to react. Here's were the **Leader-Follower** approach comes in handy: our little dude will temporarily claim leadership, the addressed peers will act as followers. The specifics on how this transition happens are a little bit more complicated and context related, but the basics are those. And because we'll define the `Rallying` state as that which corresponds to a client being in the needed position, which in itself is the tile with the needed resources for the little dude to level up, we'll also have to add two other states that 1) act as gateways to `Rallying`, and 2) enforce different behavior for leaders and followers. We'll call those, respectively, `Leading` and `MovingToRally`:
- `Leading` will be owned by a single client at any given time per-team and per-level, which means that a single team can have a leading client for the 2->3 incantation while also having some other leader for the 6->7, and so on, without caring for existing leaders in other teams.
	- This state will have the Leader be stationary until:
		- Food goes below critical threshold, in which case the Rally will disband and the specific level leadership will be back for claims
		- Enough peers responding to the rally call arrive to its tile, in which case it will go into `Rally` state and, quickly, into `Incantation` state, which will trigger the level up process for all participants.
		- Also, and because the required follower amount will always take some time, the Leader will also use those in-between ticks to call `pose()` and place stones in the incantation tile.
- `MovingToRally` will be the state related to the Follower status. Every client in the same team and level as the promoted Leader will respond to the call for a Rally, turn into a follower and move in the *direction* of the leaders broadcasts, all of it enclosed in some restrictions and checks:
	- This transition will only happen if the set up food thresholds are met. These are in place (although with different values) both for Leaders and Followers, and they are needed because as the levels go up, the incantations become more restrictive and, therefore, need more time. And in *Zappy*, time means food, so it wouldn't make sense for a Leader or a Follower to take up any of the related states if they're not going to be able to sustain them for enough time to give some assurance that the actual incantation *can* take place.
	- Followers will use the same mechanism as Leaders use to call for Rally, the `broadcast` pipeline. While leaders call this function with a `RALLY` value, which everyone in the game receives and, after checking that they qualify as target (correct level and team), react to, Followers broadcat `HERE` when they arrive to the Leader's tile. They know this is the case because the computation of the `direction` value, done in the server, returns 0, at which point they **inform about it JUST ONCE** and: 1) transition to `Rallying` state, 2) wait for other peers or for a critical food situation, 3) level up if everything goes fine. If not, they'll just get disbanded and go back to the baseline, `CollectingFood` state.

With this two set ups, Rallies can happen, and if finely tuned there will never be a case in which leadership is contentious. Only if by extreme chance two same team and level clients try to become leaders at the same time those calls might be answered at the some-ish time by the server and break things, but that's easily fixed by time-jittering a little bit the exact instant in which leadership is claimed in the client side. The key stuff here is that **a client either finds itself in the conditions needed to become a leader or gets called as follower before that**. The `Rally` calls that trigger the transition to Follower only happen if there is a Leader, and Leaders register the amount of same-tile peers by reacting to `HERE` receptions. A time buffer happens in the intraction of the `tick()` and the `broadcast()` timings, but that's just something that we have to assume and be at peace with some high level incantations not succedding on first try. Something that, on the other hand, is just rational and makes for a more interesting game, giving way to possibility for every team beyond the one who came first.

Implementing this is an incremental process. My curent Behavior class has around 17 flags, around 9 time trackers, and a handful of counters, which might be a collection that could use some cleanup, almost surely, but I say this because attempting to place all of this at once would be insane. While writing the code, one finds themselves in need for this or that attribute, so once again: take it slow, go little by little. My finished functions work around timeouts, check-oriented flags and condition tracking integers, and at this point I think we should once again try to look at things from a couple of steps back and talk a little bit about **Finite State Machines**, their design, how to wrap our minds around them and, maybe most importantly, how to communicate about them. And for that we're going to have to go into the magic world of F L O W  D I A G R A M S.

I guess I should say at this point that coding the AI client for *Zappy* around a FSM is a good way to get ourselves close to their limitations. It's not that this is a game that requires a complex protocol, but as routines cristalized and transitions piled on top of each others I could feel a certain tightness slowly creeping in. Maybe this is all because I have a not-that-good FSM design, which I'll let you tell me if that is the case, but the matter of the fact is that my current layout, which can be conceptualized as a **Finite State Machine with event-driven transitions and some side effects**, has multiple kinds of transitions (with some *"hidden"* ones), hierarchichal behavior and opportunistic side tracking possibilities, which all together kind of signal towards a **behavior tree**, which we are NOT going to follow at this moment of our lives. It is just worth noting, nothing else, and someday, somehow, I'll get into them.

Anyways, let's get diagramatical.

