# 4.2 - Going With the Flowing

> *The diagrams in this sections might be ASCII placeholders for something more elaborated. I hope I find enough time to make better ones after writing the document, but if you still find them in their barebones conditions: sorry about that! After all, the information is the same ;D*

I know this will come as a great shock to *thou who art reading*, but drawing flow charts and diagrams can be kind of hard. Well, maybe not *hard*, but something that can easily become convoluted, but luckily for us something that can be quite fun and that is kind of a neccesary step towards proofing your pudding. Flow diagrams are very, very useful for both the design process and for the review stages of mesh-y codebases development like our AI's behavior. At the very least is going to be a nice addition to your toolkit, and something which will help you a lot when working in shared codebases. In the case of my proposed AI behavior, we could make several considerations regarding what information we should translate into diagrams. To me, this piece of code could use:
- A) **Core Loop Diagram**, one that embodies the general functioning of the FSM, showing how the decision making works and is processed in a **gameplay loop representation**
- B) **Rally System Specifics**, so that we can separate everything related to coordination around broadcast events, this being a *system diagram* capturing the multiplayer coordination system
- C) **Global Rules Structure**, as to make visible every implicit derivation and transition between states that occur due to conditions such as not-passed checks, participation constrains, etc, more in the form of a **rule listing** than an arrow-based scheme.

So, let's start with the core structure. The following diagram is something that should be in mind from the very beginning of the design process, that moment in which we sit down, put ourselves in front of the first decision making layout and need to trace a structure that will then be turned into code and, then, back into a diagram. For me, the **Core Loop Diagram** of my FMS, looks like this:
```
          +------------------+
    +---> |   CollectFood    |
    |     +------------------+
    |             ↓
    |     +------------------+
    +-----|  CollectStones   | <-------------+
          +------------------+               |
                   ↓                         |
          +------------------+               |
          | ClaimingLeader   |               |
          +------------------+               |
           ↓ success     ↓ fail              |
      +---------+     +------------------+   |
      | Leading |     | MovingToRally    |   |
      +---------+     +------------------+   |
           ↓                |                |
        Rallying <----------+                |
           ↓                                 |
      Incantating ---------------------------+
```
This loop guarantees:
- survival (food)
- progression (stones → level)
- coordination (rally → incantation)

It doesn't have the forking sub-routine, but that's secondary to the core loop, so we can safely say that *this is it*, the irreducible loop, the minimal cycle that guarantees progression. Clients gather resources, move into the rallying protocol by acquiring roles, try to level up, go back to resource gathering. Forever, and ever, until they die, they win, or the computer running the simulation explodes. The crucial aspect here is that there is a **visible circularity**, whithout which our FSM would not work in, well, a loop. The flow starts at the top and, from there, you can follow any arrowed path of your choice and you will never stop moving, which is how we need the AI to work. The only exception to this would be the exit point, the `Idle` state that's not part of the flow *per se* but just the state in which the clients go when the loop is broken, which will only happen if the game is won by a team. It is not really necessary, but I need to set up a way to enter a possible end-game state when every part of the project is done and wired, so that a display of statistics with a the final state of the players and the game arena can be (re)viewed, for example. Irrelevant for now!

By having this pinned down to the brain, one can have a fallback document if things go south: you might be missing the *how*, but you can be sure about the *what*, and that's the best safenet there is. The next step would be to design the code structure that can sustain this flow, which in my case is done, under the hood, by laying out the specific collection of needed states and the interfacing mechanism. Regarding the first, I devised the following:

```cpp
AIState::CollectFood
AIState::CollectStones
AIState::ClaimingLeader
AIState::Leading
AIState::MovingToRally
AIState::Rallying
AIState::Incantating
AIState::Forking
AIState::WaitingForHatch
AIState::Idle
```
Regarding the second, what I wrote was somewhat of a `tick()` orchestrator that delegated the flow to specific, state-related sub-ticking functions. There's a central loop that acts as the **FSM driver** and there are distinct logic pathways, peppered with some event-driven transitions, so we can say that this is **tick-based Finite State Machine reacting to external events**, which could qualify as a simple game AI (every long journey starts with a single step!). So, with the driver in place, the next thing to do is design the hierarchical behavior and its layers, which in my case can be divided in:
- **Survival layer**, made up of `CollectFood`, the bottom-most fallback state in which clients focus solely in avoiding death by collecting food
- **Progression layer**, made up of `CollectStones` and `Incantating`, sandwiching the social related states, in which clients colaborate to advance in the game
- **Social layer**, made up of `ClaimingLeader`, `Leading`, `MovingToRally` and `Rallying`, all of them conforming the scaffolding of cooperation and inter-client communication, which makes little dudes be able to talk and answer to each other
- **Reproduction layer**, made up of `Forking` and `WaitingForHatch`, more like a side-line layer, an occasional deviation from the main loop that lets clients extend or replenish their teams.

Because the **surviving layer** is both the entry point and the safest fallback, it's easy to identify that the strongest constrain regarding state transitions should be **food amounts**. Depending on the specific transition, additional checks and requirements will be in place, but if food-related gateways are not carefully managed the clients are most likely to end up 1) dying, 2) constantly looping back to surviving without achieving anything beyond keeping themselves alive. In *Zappy*, time rules, authors, imposes certain rythms, and because commands have execution delays and every action is composed of multiple commands, any little dude that sets a little objective for itself is going to need, well, time, and because time in our context is meassured in food, there you have it. Even collecting food needs food, so a critical lower-clamp is paramount to avoid starvation to the best of our capabilities (some, sadly, will always die, nothing we can do about it, well, maybe having quite a high critical food threshold, which would get in the way of PROGRESS and will make things very difficult for any little dude that aspires to be SOMEONE IN LIFE, so I guess it all boils down to a choice: do you want to eat or do you want to succeed???). Food thresholds are set up via class constants, with values that need to come from continuous testing in order to find those that give to your AI the correct equilibrium between living and working. This is starting to become sad. Am I a little dude? Was *The Matrix* a live-action adaptation of the world renowned game classic *Zappy*? I, as Neo did towards the end of the first movie, am starting to believe.

Moving on, and without getting into the specificities of each logic path, here's the heart of the FMS:
```cpp
void Behavior::tick(int64_t nowMs) {
	_lastTickMs = nowMs;
	if (hasCommandInFlight()) return;

	if (nowMs - _lastInventoryRefreshMs > 5000) {
		_lastInventoryRefreshMs = nowMs;
		if (!isInventoryStale() && !hasCommandInFlight()) {
			refreshInventory();
			return;
		}
	}

	if (_aiState == AIState::MovingToRally) {

		bool navJustDrained = _navPlan.empty() && _waitingForBroadcast;
		if (isVisionStale() && (navJustDrained || nowMs - _lastMovingToRallyVisionMs > 2000)) {
			_lastMovingToRallyVisionMs = nowMs;
			refreshVision();
			return;
		}
	} else {
		if (isVisionStale()) { refreshVision(); return; }
	}

	if (isInventoryStale()) { refreshInventory(); return; }

	switch (_aiState) {
		case AIState::CollectFood:      tickCollectFood();				break;
		case AIState::CollectStones:    tickCollectStones();			break;
		case AIState::Incantating:      tickIncantating();				break;
		case AIState::ClaimingLeader:   tickClaimingLeader();			break;
		case AIState::Leading:          tickLeading(nowMs);				break;
		case AIState::MovingToRally:    tickMovingToRally(nowMs);		break;
		case AIState::Rallying:         tickRallying(nowMs);			break;
		case AIState::Forking:			tickForking();					break;
		case AIState::WaitingForHatch:	tickWaitingForHatch(nowMs);		break;
		case AIState::Idle:             tickIdle();						break;
	}
}
```

Divided in steps, this:
- Updates the time tracker `nowMs`
- Triggers periodic inventory refresh calls
- Triggers vision refresh under specific conditions
- Drives the flow forward to the switch base `AIState` delegator

From here, development really is just a (not-so-simple) matter of coding the behavior sub-protocol for every state and correctly place transitions between them. To do so, I've learned (the hard way) that these considerations must be taken:
- Every state is most likely going to need an array of checks in their entry point, before their specific, local initializations. Generally speaking, these checks detect a collection of situations that shoud either trigger a state transition or trigger a specific micro-pathway in the current state. Things like "is this my first tick in this state?", "do I have enough food to keep doing what I'm doing?", "have I gone through the my current instruction?", "are there any opportunity-related decisions I should think about?", ... Every state will slightly defer, all while some constrains being somewhat universal, like critical food level triggers, which in themselves will again have different consequences depending on the particular state. And, obviously, when juggling all of these, you'll most likely hit a thousand walls, so my best advice here would be to **build in progressive circles**. I, for example, aimed for a first testable client being able to move around and gather food. Nothing more, nothing less, enough to start putting both `Behavior` and `Navigator` against the ropes, calling a big portion of the overall commands that *Zappy* AI clients need, and easily reviewable. My first probes just returned an infinite chain of logs of a single little dude ever-growing its food reserves. From there, that initial loop can be extended: let's also make it pick up stones; then we'll have it pick up specific stones related to the current level of the client; then, we'll introduce the placing-in-tile command and close the basis of the game loop;... Then a second circle, related to the social layer, one that can first be unit-tested and then progressively implemented in the probe-based test. And so on and so forth. There's absolutely no way of writing this perfectly on first try (well, maybe a senior developer can, what do I know), so the mindset here is: organize to avoid mistakes going ham. Bind yourself to the building procedures we've been following up until this point: identify your next, little objective, then lay it down logically, proceed to coding it, finally test it. Little steps require little time to make little fixes. And so goes the other way around.

